This directory contains a version of HORSE that uses lambda expressions
for lists and structures, curses for I/O, and works on UNIX machines, too.

16 Oct
Output logging added.
Debug output supression added.
Bugs with letrec corrected.  Binding offset was not maintained correctly;
  also, incorrect env pointer was loaded when expanding a REC instruction.
Output history added.

17 Oct
Fixed bug in parser.  When LET, LET*, or LETREC was in the head position,
  any non-atomic args were not parsed.
Added file name and line number to parser error reporting.
Changed reductions, red_limit, total_reductions to be unsigned longs so
  that more reductions can occur before reductions == red_limit.

18 Oct
Fixed bug in parser. YYVAL.INTVAL declaration changed from int to long.
  The parser was incorrectly reading in large fixnum values.
Bug with letrec corrected.  When a REC is being expanded, the env value
  should be pushed on the stack *after* all ubv's have been pushed into
  the env.

1 Nov
Working on a new EQUAL predicate.
Improved the user screen output while in SUPRESS mode.

6 Nov
New EQUAL predicate completed.
Fixed IF primitive so that the cut back is from primitive's address, and
  not WS.  This makes sure IF's memory management works even if its 
  conditional's memory management doesn't.
Changed outstring stuff to check for CTRL-C user interrupt on IBM.
Changed AND and OR primitives to evaluate all their arguments, if
  necessary, to try and come up with a boolean answer.  Any non-boolean
  args are carried along.  Previously, evaluation stopped when a non-boolean
  arg was found.


7 Nov
Begin adding unification, which requires lots of basic changes to HORSE:
Add a UNIFY and UNIFY* instruction.
A reset-list is added, to keep track of unification bindings so they
	can be undone if necessary.
IP's (invisible pointers) are used to link value cells bound together 
	by unification. (This makes it necessary to modify LOOKUP to chase
	down IP's).
LAMBDA in problem mode will push a pointer to its env entry if it is unbound, 
	and in result mode will check to see if a value has been assigned via
	unification.  If it has, the LAMBDA will dissappear by turning into
	a no-op.
UBV's in the graph are now called  EP's (environment pointers), and they
	point to the value cell.  This meant changes to UBV, VAR, LAMBDA, and
	EQUAL.
Changed MAIN so that when reducing an old focus expression, the input
	expression comes from parsing the textual output of the previous
	reduction result.  This is instead of copying and shifting the graph.
	This change was neccessary to overcome binding offset problems caused
	by lambdas that dissappeared during unification.  How can you tell
	which unbound variables know to be decremented without re-traversing
	the graph?  You can't, so I am letting the printer & parser do the work.
AND primitive changed to reset reset-list if its value is FALSE.
A binding mechanism must be added to AND and OR to preserve unified values
	if the AND or OR runs out of reductions before it completes.
OR primitive changed to reset reset-list if there is no answer of TRUE.
NOT primitive changed to reset reset-list if changing true to false.


14 Nov
A problem arising from unification:  it is no longer permissible to simply
cut back the environment after a JOIN.  For just functional programming,
it's OK to do this because all bindings are done through beta reductions and 
we know the environment at any given point will not need to expand.  With 
unification bindings, however, we don't know if new closures will be made
for old variables.  Therefore, we risk loosing new variable bindings for
closures that were stuck on top of freespace.
My tenetative solution is to make UBV's 3 words long, so that closures will
fit in them.


18 Nov
Modified printer to wrap body of LET, LAMBDA, LET* and LETREC's in a pair
	of parens, so that printed expression could be parsed correctly.
Added the primitives FLOOR, CEILING, and EXPT.


21 Nov
Because unification requires that reduced subgraphs be kept around for
later use, these graphs are copied over into a HEAP area above the 
environment.  This is strictly a kludge.  The COPY_GRAPH procedure will
be used; it needed to be recovered from archived versions of HORSE.

The primitives AND should reset the heap pointer to its original value
upon failure.


5 Dec
Rewriting UNIFY and UNIFY* so that UNIFY becomes a PRIM_2.  This helps
	consistency, which I really need right now.
LAMBDA was changed back so that UBV's only take one memory space.  If a 
	closure needs to be constructed, it can be done in the HEAP area.

6 Dec
Trying to figure out how to handle existential variables that need their
	scope broadened.  Have modified OCCURS to mark those variables needing
	to be lifted and to keep track of how far they need to be moved.
